%N A399690 Numerator of delta(n), the density of the integers that are divisible by some integer in the open interval (n, 2n).
%C A399690 The set of integers having a divisor in the open interval (n, 2n) is a union of residue classes modulo L(n) = lcm(n+1, ..., 2n-1); hence delta(n) is a rational number, given by the finite inclusion-exclusion delta(n) = 1 - Sum_{S subset of {n+1..2n-1}} (-1)^|S|/lcm(S), the empty set contributing 1. The same holds for delta_r(n), the density of integers having exactly r divisors in (n, 2n).
%C A399690 This is the function delta(n) of Erdős Problem 446. Besicovitch (1934) proved liminf delta(n) = 0; Erdős (1935) proved delta(n) = o(1); Erdős (1960) proved delta(n) = (log n)^(-alpha+o(1)), where alpha = 1 - (1 + log log 2)/log 2 = 0.0860713... is the Erdős-Tenenbaum-Ford constant (A074738); Tenenbaum (1984) refined this; and Ford (2008) determined the true order of magnitude, delta(n) asymptotic to 1/((log n)^alpha (log log n)^(3/2)).
%C A399690 Convention: the interval is open at both ends, as in Erdős's question. Replacing (n,2n) by (n,2n] or by [n,2n) changes every term but not the asymptotics, since the sets of admissible divisors differ by at most the single element 2n or n, so that the two densities differ by at most 1/n = o(delta(n)).
%C A399690 delta(n) is not monotonic. For 2 <= n <= 40 one has delta(n) < delta(n-1) exactly for n = 5, 7, 11, 13, 17, 19, 23, 25, 26, 29, 31, 33, 35, 37, 38, 39; this list contains every prime n with 5 <= n <= 37.
%C A399690 delta(7) = delta(8) = 181/429 is the only coincidence delta(n) = delta(m) with 1 <= n < m <= 40. The two underlying sets are nevertheless distinct: 8 has a divisor in (7,14), namely 8, but none in (8,16), while 14 has a divisor in (8,16), namely 14, but none in (7,14). The coincidence is specific to the open convention: for (n,2n] one gets delta(7) = 1355/3003 and delta(8) = 1339/3003.
%D A399690 A. S. Besicovitch, On the density of certain sequences of integers, J. London Math. Soc. 9 (1934), 2-6.
%D A399690 R. R. Hall and G. Tenenbaum, Divisors, Cambridge Tracts in Mathematics 90, Cambridge Univ. Press, 1988.
%H A399690 Hassane Bakkaoui, <a href="/A399690/b399690.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..40</a>
%H A399690 T. F. Bloom, <a href="https://www.erdosproblems.com/446">Erdős Problem 446</a>
%H A399690 K. Ford, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0401223">The distribution of integers with a divisor in a given interval</a>, arXiv:math/0401223 [math.NT], 2004-2008.
%H A399690 G. Tenenbaum, <a href="http://www.numdam.org/item?id=CM_1984__51_2_243_0">Sur la probabilité qu'un entier possède un diviseur dans un intervalle donné</a>, Compositio Math. 51 (1984), 243-263.
%e A399690 delta(4) = 3/7: the integers in (4,8) are 5, 6, 7, and inclusion-exclusion gives 1/5 + 1/6 + 1/7 - 1/30 - 1/35 - 1/42 + 1/210 = 3/7, so a(4) = 3.
%e A399690 The fractions begin 0, 1/3, 2/5, 3/7, 17/42, 103/231, 181/429, 181/429, ...
%o A399690 (Python)
%o A399690 from fractions import Fraction
%o A399690 from math import lcm
%o A399690 def delta(n):
%o A399690     dp = {1: 1} # lcm -> sum of (-1)^|S| over subsets with that lcm
%o A399690     for d in range(n+1, 2*n):
%o A399690         new = dict(dp)
%o A399690         for L, c in dp.items():
%o A399690             new[lcm(L, d)] = new.get(lcm(L, d), 0) - c
%o A399690         dp = new
%o A399690     return 1 - sum(Fraction(c, L) for L, c in dp.items())
%o A399690 def a(n): return delta(n).numerator
